Hi all, I've been reading this group for the longest time and always
found entertainment in the YAFMs and YASDs, so here goes a small story
of my own:

Dakhath the male Chaotic Elven Wizard was exploring the first four or
five levels of the dungeons, somewhat hampered by his droolingly
stupid, cowardly, corridor-blocking kitten, which simply refused to
follow him more than ten steps in any direction or kill anything more
dangerous than a lichen, and eventually got left behind on level three.
 He felt a little pang of jealously for all the other elves (no doubt
with higher St and Ch) whose cats apparently functioned like they
promised in the advertisements.

Which reminded him.  A scroll of destroy armor? A wand of locking?
What kind of gear was this?  Was Neferet the Green going senile, or is
he the beneficiary of a large insurance policy in my name?  At least he
handed out this spellbook of magic missile and potion of healing.

So we come to the Gnomish mines.  At the first wary watchful step,
Dakhath was pelted with seventy-three darts, crossbow bolts, thrown
daggers, potions, etc.  Gnome households must be lively places indeed.
(The Gnomish Housewife hurls a plate of spaghetti!--More--
The Gnomish Housewife hurls a vaccuum cleaner!)

One of these daggers in Elven!  Is this standard issue on all Gnomish
miners now?  How could they get away with giving Dakhath this
(admittedly blessed and enchanted) TREE BRANCH when Johnny Gnomish down
underground gets a rustproof, throwable, stackable, one-handed weapon?
Well, Dakhath hereby claims this elven dagger, and thank you very much,
Neferet.

A few levels down, we become aware of a situation.  Lacking color, it
is shown below in hopefully intuitive ASCII.  On the left we have
Dakhath. On the right, there are the up stairs, a Gnome, a spawn of
hill orcs, and the humor begins to be apparent when I tell you that the
^ is a polymorph trap, which my handy cloak of magic resistance let me
stumble on through.

  -----------     --------
 /           \___/ <
|@                ^   ooooG
 \___________/----\______


As Dakhath stared from his immobile vantage point in a friendly bear
trap, there emerged a baby yellow dragon, a baby red dragon, a hill
giant, a yeti, and a gray dragon.  Having now increased their threat
level by a conservatively-estimated factor of one million, the mob now
did the only sensible thing.  They ran through the gap and scattered
completely out of their pleasing, magic missile-able line.  They also
completely refused to line up more than two at a time and for more than
one turn.

There was dancing, singing (ahem... a string of swears can pass for a
song nowadays), waving of wands at floors to engrave certain words, and
finally a dash up the stairs, leaving a red, a yellow, and a white
corpse behind.  The giant fled to some dark part of the mine.  All that
was left was the fully-grown gray dragon.

Pudding farmers have got nothing on a low-level Wizard trying to kill
an adult gray dragon.  While I'm pretty sure Nethack informed me that
Dakhath was responsible for the gray dragon's death, Dakhath, the
dragon, and Nethack itself of us know that it really died of boredom.

After that, the hill giant was tracked down and killed, and its boulder
pushed over the polymorph trap.  That solves that.  So down we go.
Minetown was a particularly boring reward, no lamps, cross-aligned
temple etc. so it was down we go again.

The next level below minetown showed Dakhath that if it looks like a
dwarf king, and walks like a dwarf king, it is probably a master mind
flayer.  It is well that Dakhath has a freakishly large container for
his 20 In, as he soon felt very, very stupid and ran back upstairs.
However, even horribly enfeebled in this way, he still remembered that
applying the horn of a unicorn to certain wounds will make him feel
better.

So what we have here is a master of arcane magics jamming a two-foot
piece of bone into his head to become smarter.  It is probably best not
to inquire into the actual process of unicorn horn healing.  Somewhere,
a bandaged gray horse is laughing.

Well, that was enough of the mines for Dakhath.  The dungeons above are
looking a lot more attractive now.  So back up we go.  Oh yes, I feel
like I have been here before, but the bits of me that hold maps of this
area are in the belly of a squid wearing a dwarf costume two levels
down.

Moving an errant boulder aside, Dakhath feels a change coming over him.
 It is probably for the best that he has no memory of this particular
trap giving him HOURS of grief.  So, having blown its luck on creating
an adult gray dragon, the trap kindly turns Dakhath into a jellyfish,
which he remains as until he is 'rescued' by a passing centipede.

Mapping out the first few levels of the dungeons for the second time,
he is attacked ferociously by a familiar looking kitten.

He later perished on the Rogue level when a troll he was eating got up
again and hit him for (I swear) thirty-one damage.



